KenseiECS test stand, module unity: mutant for Stand/unity/run.sh --selftest (lib/selftest.py).
Paths are relative to the package folder (KenseiECS/): patch -p1 -d <copy of KenseiECS> < rollback-error-directive.patch

Mutant: rollback-error-directive
Kind: bug
Origin: rollback of a 2.2.1 fix (content builds with Debug Mode on work again)
Expect: content-build-debug-mode, dev-build-not-checked
Cases: S-None;AA-DevDefault
Why: DevTools/KenseiDebugReleaseCheck.cs gets back the #error of 2.2.0: every content build with Debug Mode on, and the content step of a Development player build with Addressables' default settings, fails (known defect U1).

--- a/DevTools/KenseiDebugReleaseCheck.cs
+++ b/DevTools/KenseiDebugReleaseCheck.cs
@@ -1,20 +1,9 @@
-// Marker for the release check after the player scripts compile (Editor/KenseiDebugPlayerScriptsCheck).
-// The preprocess guard (KenseiDebugBuildGuard) reads the Scripting Define Symbols of the active Build
-// Profile and Player Settings only; a player build can also get KENSEI_DEBUG from
-// BuildPlayerOptions.extraScriptingDefines, Player Settings -> Additional Compiler Arguments or an
-// Assets/csc.rsp. The player compile sees every define source, so a compile with the define contains
-// this type, and the editor check fails a non-development player build whose KenseiECS.dll has it.
-// Whether the build is a development one is read from the build options there, like the guard does,
-// not from DEVELOPMENT_BUILD (deprecated in Unity 6.6).
-// Content builds (Addressables, the Scriptable Build Pipeline, AssetBundles) compile the same player
-// scripts, but Unity never runs that check for them, so Debug Mode does not stop them. 2.2.0 had an
-// #error here, which failed every content build while Debug Mode was on.
-// UNITY_5_3_OR_NEWER keeps the marker to Unity compiles: the .NET test build defines KENSEI_DEBUG
-// without UNITY_EDITOR on purpose.
-#if UNITY_5_3_OR_NEWER && KENSEI_DEBUG && !UNITY_EDITOR && !KENSEI_ALLOW_DEBUG_RELEASE
-namespace KenseiECS.Internal {
-    // The name is what the editor check looks for (KenseiDebugDefines.RELEASE_MARKER); it must not
-    // appear anywhere else in the runtime assembly.
-    internal static class KenseiDebugInReleasePlayer { }
-}
+// Compile-time backstop for KenseiDebugBuildGuard (Editor). The guard reads the defines of the
+// active Build Profile and Player Settings only; a player build can also get KENSEI_DEBUG from
+// BuildPlayerOptions.extraScriptingDefines or an Assets/csc.rsp, which the guard cannot see. The
+// player compile sees every define source, so a release player with the define fails here.
+// UNITY_5_3_OR_NEWER keeps the check to Unity compiles: the .NET test build defines KENSEI_DEBUG
+// without UNITY_EDITOR or DEVELOPMENT_BUILD on purpose.
+#if UNITY_5_3_OR_NEWER && KENSEI_DEBUG && !UNITY_EDITOR && !DEVELOPMENT_BUILD && !KENSEI_ALLOW_DEBUG_RELEASE
+#error KenseiECS: KENSEI_DEBUG is defined in a release (non-development) player build; it compiles validation and debug tooling into the player. Remove it from every define source (Build Profile, Player Settings, BuildPlayerOptions.extraScriptingDefines, csc.rsp), tick Development Build, or add KENSEI_ALLOW_DEBUG_RELEASE to ship it on purpose.
 #endif
